								<td align="left" valign="middle">
									<b><a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?topic=1453.msg15155#msg15155">Re: Server Merges Starting</a></b><br />
									<span class="smalltext">&#171; <b>Reply #30 on:</b> March 01, 2005, 11:20:56 am &#187;</span></td>
								<td align="right" valign="bottom" height="20" nowrap="nowrap" style="font-size: smaller;">
								</td>
							</tr></table>
							<hr width="100%" size="1" class="hrcolor" />
							I would actually expect Rodet to get a merger with a low population server soonish. That&#039;s the pattern they will tend to follow, lower pop + higher pop = stop pop.... ok lame but wtf... heh<br /><br />This isn&#039;t a sky is falling subject, but anyone who denies that EQ has lost favor in the MMORPG realm is kidding themselves.<br /><br />It&#039;s also time to wake up and take a look at this from a business perspective. SoE has always made more money from Expansions than from subscriptions. I tend to suspect, but can not prove that expansions are what kept the subscription rates so low for so long, as well as allowed them to offer package subscriptions.<br /><br />Someone with an all access pass obviously does not pay the same rates per game as do those who pay straight for one game, and I suspect many people have taken at least one all access pass to be able to mess around with the new games at times.<br /><br />This is cutting into the monthy revenue to support the servers, so even EQ2 has had to offer Adventure Packs, to I assume support the costs of the game, and provide real profit for the share holders. I am fairly positive that is why EQ sees an expansion every 6 months now.<br /><br />It would also tend to be supported with the changes in how new content comes to EQ, it used to be you got alot more new zones/revamps/features rolled out on a regular basis when they had no combines subscription plans etc... Expansions really were expansions to the game, and not scheduled to be popped out every 6 months like clockwork. I really think expansions on the order of Luclin, Kunark, Velious, and PoP show the amount of work a real expansion takes, versus the 6 month bang jobs we see now.<br /><br />So my thoery is, that at this point loosing a subscriber is a small bit, but loosing that continual expansion buyer is a bigger hit to thier wallets, and that is what prompted server merges just after an expansion release.<br /><br />Please, for those of a more dramatic nature this is not a doom and gloom post about EQ either, this is simple analysis, please offer replies with thoughts along those lines. 
