							<hr width="100%" size="1" class="hrcolor" />
							Koruption, I think what you&#039;re describing is what happens when any game really hits the mainstream.  EQ started with most of the population being dedicated RPGers.  It was D&amp;D brought to the computer screen like never before, or at least a great new thing for long time computer RPGers.  In other words you had a high proportion of mature gamers (whatever their age).  Then it hit the big time and the average maturity of the gamers and how dedicated they were to an RPG format fell hard.  <br /><br /><div class="quoteheader">Quote</div><div class="quote">Right now, playing WoW is a little like Diablo2 in the personality department, no one is really worried about their rep among other players on the server.  They dont care if they KS, Camp steal, Hack, Dupe, Cheat, or swindle.  I know a lot of them are young adults that were just looking for the Next Thing, and dont know how seriously some of us take a MMORPG.  <br /></div><br />I don&#039;t have firsthand experience with WoW to judge, but if this statement is accurate, well, it doesn&#039;t surprise me.  It&#039;s a combination of the game hitting the mainstream and not just the dedicated RPGers, plus that younger b.net kiddy crowd we all love so much.  I don&#039;t know if my previous prediction will be born out in practice or not, but it may be something EQ1 has to its advantage in that the gamers who are left will tend to be more mature because a higher percentage of the less mature ones want the next big thing.  Of course those left may just be the more casual gamers too.  *shrug*
